# Pixi.js Performance

Optimize for smooth 60fps on web and Capacitor. Prefer WebGPU, fall back to WebGL. Drive the game loop with Pixi's ticker — do not roll a custom `requestAnimationFrame` loop.

## Rendering

- Batch sprites; nest containers only when the transform hierarchy needs it.
- Use texture atlases to cut texture swaps. Use `ParticleContainer` for large sets of similar sprites.
- Cull off-screen objects. Cache complex graphics. Be wary of filters (especially on mobile).
- Use Pixi's interaction manager; do not attach raw DOM listeners to the canvas.
- Stay under the ~65k vertices Graphics limit; split or use sprites/meshes when you would exceed it.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — allocate and add children every tick
app.ticker.add(() => {
  const burst = new Sprite(texture);
  world.addChild(burst);
});

// ✅ GOOD — pool, reuse, cull
function spawnBullet(pool: Sprite[], texture: Texture): Sprite {
  const bullet = pool.pop() ?? new Sprite(texture);
  bullet.visible = true;
  return bullet;
}
```

## Runtime cost

- Minimize allocations during gameplay to avoid GC pauses. Pool frequently created/destroyed objects and remove unused scene-graph nodes.
- Stream or chunk large worlds. Lazy-load and prefetch critical assets; compress and serve lower-res textures on older devices.
- Cache values reused across frames (bounds, lookups). Keep draw order simple.
- Capacitor: optimize touch/gestures, responsive layout, asset size, and battery. Use `legacy: true` only for old devices. Centralize native plugins.

## Shipping

- Cache static assets on the CDN (Vercel/Cloudflare). Progressive-load so first interaction is fast.
- Add a library only if it is maintained, typed, platform-compatible, and cheaper than in-house code. Profile before and after.
